    text: "How to verify an original laboratory report\n\nOpen a published PSL report, confirm it matches your lot, and use the laboratory's verification tools when available.\n\nPublished PSL batch documentation is the **original third-party laboratory report** for a specific sample—not a pass/fail badge or in-house summary.\n\n## What “original report” means\n\n- Issued by a laboratory independent of PSL\n- Tied to a named batch, task number, and sample\n- Shown as the laboratory's document (image or PDF), linked from PSL pages\n\nIn-house or supplier-only paperwork is not the same as a published third-party report.\n\n## Find your report on PSL\n\n1. Note the lot or batch on your vial label.\n2. Visit the product page or **COA / Batch Lookup** (`/coa`).\n3. Search by task number or batch name when lookup is available.\n4. Open the linked report and confirm the batch and task number match your label.\n\nIf no report is published for your lot, contact support@psllabs.org.\n\n## Verify with the testing laboratory\n\nWhen the report includes a verification URL or key (Janoshik reports include a verification key on the document):\n\n1. Open the original report from PSL.\n2. Follow the laboratory's verification instructions on that report.\n3. Enter the key exactly as printed on the **original** document.\n\nVerification confirms the report file matches the laboratory's records. It does not extend results to other lots.\n\n## What to record\n\nFor laboratory record-keeping, note:\n\n- Product name and SKU (when shown)\n- Batch name and task number\n- Analysis date on the report\n- Identity, amount, and purity **only as printed** on that report\n- That results apply solely to the tested sample identified\n\nTesting scope and results are shown on each original laboratory report. Review the report itself for the complete scope of testing performed.\n\n## Results apply only to the tested batch\n\nA report documents one tested sample. It does not describe other lots, future production, or materials not identified in that report.\n\nFor help locating documentation: support@psllabs.org.",
